  <!--
    Ayg 1.3 front head + LiDAR.

    Replaces camera.xacro (Intel RealSense D555). The 1.3 chassis carries a HESAI
    JT128 on a new front mount; ayg_lidar drives it (see ros/src/ayg_lidar) and
    stamps clouds in `lidar_link`.

    The `Head` link is the mount, taken verbatim from the SolidWorks export
    CF-AYG-1.3-00-000-URDF-6. Its 0.72713 kg ALREADY INCLUDES the LiDAR body
    (confirmed with the mechanical engineer) - do not add sensor mass on top.

    The link is named `Head` (the export's name), not `Camera` as the D555 era
    used. Anything that referenced the old body name must follow - notably
    cf_lab's head-contact termination (body_names="Camera").
  -->
  <!-- ============================== Head ============================== -->
  <link name="Head">
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.14526 0.0015227 -0.038347"/>
      <mass value="0.72713"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00048304" ixy="5.642E-09" ixz="2.7327E-05" iyy="0.0012008" iyz="4.3319E-09" izz="0.001352"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry>
        <mesh filename="package://ayg_description/meshes/Head.STL"/>
      </geometry>
      <material name="">
        <color rgba="0.69804 0.69804 0.69804 1"/>
      </material>
    </visual>
    <!--
      Collision as two primitives, each shaped like the thing it stands for: a
      box for the carry handle on top, a cylinder for the LiDAR below it. The
      head mesh fills only 10.4% of its 0.226 x 0.134 x 0.116 bounding box (it
      is an open bracket cage), so a single wrapping box would claim a great
      deal of empty space on a part that protrudes 0.21 m ahead of the chassis.
      Since this is the body that strikes obstacles, and near-field obstacle
      response is the open problem, shape faithfulness is worth the extra
      primitive.
      Measured sub-regions, in the Head frame:
        handle x 0.000..0.226  y +/-0.045  z  0.000..0.008
        cage   x 0.113..0.152  y +/-0.067  z -0.092..-0.006
        shroud x 0.165..0.230  y +/-0.032  z -0.093..-0.029  (coaxial with the
               spin axis; its 64.4 mm width is the CAD aperture, 64.3 mm)
      The thin strut below the handle (x 0.008..0.113, y +/-0.010) is left out:
      it is tucked under the handle box and is not a plausible contact point.
      The cage's side walls (|y| 0.033..0.067) are left out too: they are thin
      sheet either side of the sensor, and wrapping them cost a 611 cm3 box.

      The top box is the CARRY HANDLE above the LiDAR, sized to the part rather
      than to the head's bounding box: 8 mm thick (the measured plate is
      z 0.000..0.008, not the 0.0125 an earlier revision claimed) and y +/-0.045
      (the two rails and the centre spine span 0.090, not 0.110). It runs the
      full length of the head, x 0.000..0.226, back to the chassis.

      It is seated 4 mm ABOVE the measured plate, at Base-frame z 0.0540..0.0620
      rather than 0.0500..0.0580, so its top face is flush with the Base
      collision box's top surface (+0.0620). Reason: the handle's rear passes
      over the two front hips, and Head is a separate rigid body
      (merge_fixed_joints=False), so Head<->Hip is a non-adjacent pair PhysX
      leaves active - it feeds both head_contact and base_contact (the hips are
      in that termination's body list). Under HAA the hip cylinder's inboard end
      swings up to z=+0.0534, which lands inside the plate's true 0.0500..0.0580
      band; the cylinder comes up INTO the handle rather than passing under it,
      so no thinning or narrowing clears it. Lifting the box to start at 0.0540
      does, across the whole HAA range (-30..+30 deg on both front legs), with
      0.5 mm to spare over the 0.0535 boundary - and without shortening the
      handle or shrinking the hip cylinder below the coverage its exposed
      underside needs.
      The real parts never touch: Head.STL to LF_Hip.STL holds 2.0 mm at every
      HAA angle, so this is wrapper-vs-wrapper, not a hardware constraint. The
      offset trades the plate's lower 4 mm for 4 mm of air beneath the chassis
      top plane, both shielded approaches.
    -->
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.113 0 0.008"/>
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.226 0.090 0.008"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <!-- LiDAR: one cylinder on the spin axis, replacing the pod + nose boxes it
         used to take (823 -> 352 cm3). rpy sends the cylinder's own +z onto the
         axis direction (12/13, 0, -5/13); the origin is the span's midpoint
         measured along that axis from the Head origin. -->
    <collision>
      <origin rpy="0 1.965587446494658 0" xyz="0.16938461538461538 0 -0.07057692307692306"/>
      <geometry>
        <cylinder length="0.10299999999999998" radius="0.033"/>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <!-- Export Base_2_Head, verbatim. No rotation: unlike the D555 mount, which
       carried a 90 deg roll to set up the RealSense optical-frame subtree, the
       head is a plain rigid body and the export places it axis-aligned. -->
  <joint name="Base_2_Head" type="fixed">
    <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0.199 0 0.05"/>
    <parent link="Base"/>
    <child link="Head"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
  <link name="lidar_link">
    <inertial>
      <origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="1e-6"/>
      <inertia ixx="1e-9" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-9" iyz="0" izz="1e-9"/>
    </inertial>
  </link>
  <joint name="Head_2_lidar" type="fixed">
    <origin rpy="-1.965587446494658 0 -1.5707963267948966" xyz="0.1438523076923077 0.0 -0.05993846153846154"/>
    <parent link="Head"/>
    <child link="lidar_link"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>
</robot>
